#
# Flings that behave as iOS's: caught by a touch, launched at the finger's
# speed, and stacking when flicked again.
#
# Measured on the Jolla Phone with a train of ten 100 ms flicks, each
# landing while the last fling coasts, and a flick caught by a resting
# finger (Pacific's pacific-env also sets WEBKIT_KINETIC_DECEL_FRICTION to
# 2, iOS's 0.998 a millisecond):
#
# - A finger landing on a coasting page stops it at once (MayBegin to the
#   scrolling tree, which stops the kinetic animation and keeps its velocity
#   for a next flick), and the tap is not a click: a tap to stop the page
#   opened the link under it. Upstream stopped the fling only when a drag
#   passed the touch slop or 200 ms, so a resting finger let the page coast
#   on for a second. Now it stops 12-16 ms after the finger lands. The UI
#   process models the fling from the drag that started it; a caught tap
#   closes its MayBegin with Cancelled.
# - A fling launches at the finger's speed over its last 50 ms, not its
#   mean over 150 ms (a thumb still speeds up as it lets go): 0.8x the
#   finger's speed -> 1.05x. PACIFIC_FLING_VELOCITY_WINDOW_MS (0: upstream).
# - Stacked flicks compound: the animation remembered the flick's own
#   velocity, not the stacked one it ran with, so every flick of the train
#   launched at about 3300 px/s.
#
# - A finger that rests before it lifts does not fling: a drag that stopped
#   150 ms then lifted still flung 80-100 css px. Over 40 ms between the
#   last move and the lift, no fling (PACIFIC_FLING_PAUSE_MS).
# - Flicks carry as iOS's: 0.000816 |v|^1.967 of the velocity the page had
#   when the finger caught it (Flutter's measurement of iOS; 2000 px/s
#   carries 2530), not upstream's linear share of the velocity it would
#   have had if never caught; a finger that caught the page and rested
#   100 ms starts afresh (PACIFIC_FLING_CARRY_PAUSE_MS); the ceiling is
#   25000 px/s. The ten-flick train now builds 2.1, 3.8, 5.3, 9.4, 12.8,
#   then about 20 css px/ms. PACIFIC_FLING_CARRY=linear keeps upstream's.
#
# PACIFIC_FLING_TRACE=1 logs each fling's own and inherited velocity.
#
